Day 61 - Odd Even Linked List

Problem Statement:
Given the head of a singly linked list, group all the nodes with odd indices together followed by the nodes with even indices, and return the reordered list.

The first node is considered odd, the second node is even, and so on.

The relative order inside both the odd and even groups should remain the same.

Code

/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * struct ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode *next;
 *     ListNode() : val(0), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : val(x), next(next) {}
 * };
 */


class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* oddEvenList(ListNode* head) {
        if (head == NULL || head->next == NULL) {
            return head;
        }


        ListNode* odd = head;
        ListNode* even = head->next;
        ListNode* evenhead = even;


        while (even != NULL && even->next != NULL) {
            odd->next = even->next;
            odd = odd->next;


            even->next = odd->next;
            even = even->next;
        }


        odd->next = evenhead;


        return head;
    }
};




Time Complexity:
O(n)

Space Complexity:
O(1)

Approach:
The solution maintains two separate chains using odd and even pointers. odd starts at the first node and even starts at the second node. During traversal, the next pointers are rearranged so that odd-indexed nodes are connected together and even-indexed nodes are connected together. evenhead stores the first even node so that the even list can be attached after the odd list at the end. The relative order within both groups is preserved.
